<p>Chapter II runs from printed page 5 through page 18. It opens with a long poem about the Amethyst Mine crew, follows with ten underground stories from John's years mining at Creede, and ends with two full-page photographs, printed pages 17 and 18, that the table of contents does not list.</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="the-amethyst-crew">The Amethyst Crew</h2>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>My gawd, pard, that rock was hard<br>
    but we broke 'er deep and clean<br>
    the shifter's sass, the dust, the gas<br>
    the worst I've ever seen</p>

    <p>We'll wash it down when we hit town<br>
    in Tucker's Bar and Grill<br>
    they'll swear the smell is straight from hell<br>
    when we come off the hill</p>

    <p>The driver slows, looks down his nose<br>
    as we scramble with our gear<br>
    and form a line to a neon sign<br>
    that flashes "cold draft beer"</p>

    <p>Pug's at the bar and Tuck's not far<br>
    as we shuffle through the door<br>
    to take the stools of other fools<br>
    passed out around the floor</p>

    <p>The great white ape with mouth agape<br>
    has staggered from the rear<br>
    a parrot's screech stops Bernie's speech<br>
    it's cage beside a mirror</p>

    <p>A dog gone mad bursts from a pad<br>
    a monkey on it's back<br>
    the white ape's pet, his passions met<br>
    leaps up and scratches Jack</p>

    <p>Our ex-marine sounds gruff and mean<br>
    the jungle sounds remind<br>
    he learned to kill on Bougainville<br>
    it still torments his mind</p>

    <p>We yell to Pete, "your time to treat"<br>
    he orders, "pour a round"<br>
    "a toast to you and the filthiest crew<br>
    the devil ever found"</p>

    <p>"A shot and beer for our engineer"<br>
    old Paul has come in late<br>
    he strains his eyes to recognize<br>
    each worthless reprobate</p>

    <p>The stopes, the drifts, the black hole shifts<br>
    forgotten for a night<br>
    and folks will hear out lusty cheer<br>
    as Woody stands to fight</p>

    <p>The lights grow dim, Hank's face is grim<br>
    we struggle hard to hear<br>
    the name, the age, the final page<br>
    not one will shed a tear</p>

    <p>So long ago, the fables grow<br>
    as one by one we wander<br>
    to ash and grave, no souls to save<br>
    we've all been called down yonder</p>

    <p>Just one more day to earn our pay<br>
    and one more night to revel<br>
    another toast to the Happy Thought Ghost<br>
    and we'll go to meet the devil</p>

    <p>Now here we toil, far 'neath the soil<br>
    to mine old Satan's gold<br>
    we eat his tripe, our only gripe<br>
    the beer is never cold.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The poem runs across two pages, breaking after "as Woody stands to fight" (p. 5) and resuming at "The lights grow dim" (p. 6). Three slips are kept as printed: "it's cage," "on it's back," and "hear out lusty cheer." The same poem appears in "Echoes from the Thundermug," at the end of <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#miners-after-world-war-ii-a-unique-breed">"Miners After World War II: A Unique Breed,"</a> where John introduces it with a prose account of Tucker's Bar and Grill, the "great white ape," and the Amethyst Mine crew of the late 1940s. The two versions differ. The Echoes version has no stanza beginning "The stopes, the drifts, the black hole shifts"; it puts "Just one more day to earn our pay" before "So long ago, the fables grow," where this book has them the other way round; its version of that stanza reads "And one more toast to the Last Chance ghost before we meet the devil" where this book has "another toast to the Happy Thought Ghost / and we'll go to meet the devil"; and it reads "Migawd pard" and "eyes agape" where this book reads "My gawd, pard" and "mouth agape." John's memoir "Please Go to Hell" has its own roster of the Amethyst Mine crew in <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/dedication-and-the-amethyst-crew-1947/">"Dedication and The Amethyst Crew '1947.'"</a></em></p>

