<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="and-this-aint-no-bullshit">And This Ain't No Bullshit</h2>

  <p>We are deep in the bowels of the Earth. I have just mucked out, laid a set of rail and set up the pneumatic drill, called a Leyner, on column and arm that will chatter in 18 holes to blast another pile of smoking rock. It is lunch time. With my hardboil hat resting on a nail keg and a carbide lamp providing illumination I flip open my pie can and pour a cup of coffee. Red Branson has started a story. He looks in my direction and advises, "Now listen up, Pard, 'cause this here ain't no bullshit."</p>

  <p>Thirty years later I will remember that moment when Pat Madill, our helicopter pilot in minerals exploration, works his way into a story of exploration in Indonesia. "Fairy tales begin with...<em>ONCE UPON A TIME</em>...but real stories will always begin with...<em>THIS HERE AIN'T NO BULLSHIT</em>," he cautions us. Red and Pat had never met and Pat had never worked in a mine, but worker slang is universal and when a muscled fellow with sweat matted hair looks you in the eye and states, "Now listen up, Pard, 'cause this here ain't no bullshit" you had better listen and you had better believe it.</p>

  <p>We will start our stories with, "This here ain't no bullshit, but once upon a time..." and leave the rest to your judgement. So...pull up a powder box, pour a cup of coffee and we'll let Red finish his story.</p>

  <p>"They was seven of us young'uns to start with and our Pappy drug us from Oklahoma into Texas workin' the oil fields...I was a roustabout 'til I come on 18 years then I went on the pipeline crew...six of us laid 3 miles of pipe in one shift more 'n any crew before or since...pipe was so hot in that Texas sun yuh could see smoke comin' off your hands when yuh grabbed aholt of a joint...made muh hands tough as steel..looky here." With that he grabbed a large nail from a box, placed the head end in the palm of his right hand and rammed the pointed end through a 1" board he was using as a back rest. Nobody said a word so he rammed another through even further. I got up, moved my hat, and pulled two rail spikes from the keg. I walked over to some 6" x 6" ties leaning against the rib and rammed the first spike halfway into a tie with my left hand then really laid it on with my right hand and buried the second spike. Red looked at me like a bull at a bastard calf, slammed his lunch pail shut, and stormed off down the drift. Shortly after we could hear his tram car being loaded at one of the chutes.</p>

  <p>Old Bill was laughing like a madman and slapping his knee. "How the hell did you do that?" he asked. What Red didn't know and I never told him was that the ties had been leaning in that same place for well over a year. The foul air and low humidity combined to make them as soft as balsam wood though they looked like they had just come from the mill.</p>

  <p>Red didn't stay with us long but he touched my heart because he could neither read or write. He proved capable in all chores and worked with passion, but he just didn't like being underground. He drew me aside at the end of his first shift and asked if I would fill in his time ticket. It was embarrassing for both of us as he continued that none of his family had time for much schooling. That was one of many times I realized just how lucky I am to have had the opportunity for quality education in a town that cared and parents who stressed the necessity of a good education.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Neither Red Branson, "Old Bill," nor Pat Madill is named elsewhere on this site, and the book gives none of them a further identification. Red Branson and the Melvin "Red" Johnson of "Red the Medicine Man," later in this chapter, carry different surnames in the book, and nothing connects them.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="the-swede-creedes-top-miner">"The Swede" Creede's Top Miner</h2>

  <p>Sometimes we get so busy patting ourselves on the back we tend to forget the real souls of merit, the trusting people who overlooked our mistakes and molded us into productive workers and citizens.</p>

  <p>Erick W. Nelson was one of the finest miners to enter a tunnel or shaft in the Creede District. I have admired no man more than "the Swede" who rose from miner and leaser to become General Manager of Emperius Mining Company. He and my Father had been friends since childhood and I felt that friendship saved me from being fired several times when I taxed his patience. I was having problems pulling my rounds in a tight cross-cut before Erick was elevated to the top position. In desperation I drilled a sheep nose cut and overloaded the holes with explosives. I pulled the 5 feet, but I ruined two steel plats, shredded my air-hose and knocked out several lengths of pipe. I was unable to get it cleaned up and repaired before he made his inspection. Not a word was spoken, but I knew the pink slip only needed his signature.</p>

  <p>Because of Erick I was treated to one of the most awesome sights I have ever seen in a mine. All water used in the several levels of mining came from a spring between the Last Chance and Happy Thought Mines. The water was piped to the Last Chance shaft and through connected workings to the Amethyst Mine.</p>

  <p>I was driving dual inclines from a cross-cut and was just spraggin' up to drill when Erick came by. He replaced the carbide in his lamp and asked if I would help him on the water line. Every miner on level 5 was running out of water and he thought some moss or debris might have plugged the line. We took my pipe wrenches and started up 2 shaft checking every pipe joint as we climbed the rickety ladder. We arrived on the 150' level under the Sloan Tunnel, worked our way around a small cave-in, then scaled timbers to get past a larger cave, then up through a hole and onto level 2 of the Last Chance. I marveled at the way this old fellow, plagued by silicosis and rheumatics, found his way around and appeared to love every minute of his return to former haunts. We still had no water flow and continued south following the well rusted line. Erick was on his knees looking for a union to crack when I noticed a light ahead and told him someone else was on the level. He looked up, blew through his bushy moustache and said, "If that's a light, it darned sure isn't a carbide lamp." We began walking toward it and it became nearly overpowering, engulfing the entire drift.</p>

  <p>If I hadn't been in the company of this seemingly fearless Swede I would have turned tail and ran. I'm not sure, but what Erick would have been stepping on my heels. We went on....slowly....and there we were, right at what had been #2 station of the Last Chance shaft with the sun shining down a wide open hole. At some time in the night an unknown factor had cleaned the shaft of all timbers, landings and pipe, dropping the debris to create a massive jam near level 5. We had arrived at the most opportune time to have the living hell scared out of us, just as the sun was directly overhead illuminating the gaping hole. Truly the light at the end of the tunnel.</p>

  <p>The Swede took me on several trips through old workings where they mined by candle light and he learned to single jack with the best in the trade. He never forgot a thing and the knowledge gained in those early years served him and his crews well for the rest of his mining career.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>This is <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/dedication-and-the-amethyst-crew-1947/">Erick W. Nelson</a>, whom "Please Go to Hell" also calls General Manager and "one of the very finest individuals I have known." A sketch there, <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/quirks-of-the-trade/">"Quirks of the Trade,"</a> describes his "bushy moustache" and the habit of blowing up through it when he was riled, which is what he does in this story. "Echoes from the Thundermug" calls him "Erick 'Swede' Nelson," superintendent, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#jackson-recalls-building-of-ore-bins">"Jackson Recalls Building of Ore Bins,"</a> where John's father, Bill, is his foreman. The poem that opens this chapter toasts "the Happy Thought Ghost"; the Echoes version toasts "the Last Chance ghost." Both mines are named in this story.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="a-runner-in-66-raise">A "Runner" in 66 Raise</h2>

  <p>I liked everything about mining. Every smell, every sound and the excitement of seeing, firsthand, the minerals and crystals exposed with each blast while following a vein emplaced atom by atom through a complex hydro-thermal system that charged itself from a low level, mineral rich intrusive and dumped its load at an elevated level where lessening of pressure and lowering of temperature provided optimum conditions for deposition. There are other modes and models of mineral deposition, but the long term multi-phase, hydro-thermal system that filled fault fissures in the young volcanics of the Creede area provided the most spectacular crystal growths and mineral assemblages of any mining district I have had the privilege of actively mining in or visiting.</p>

  <p>Hard rock mining attracts persons from every walk of life. At various times in Creede camp we had artists, cowboys, dentist, lawyers, farmers, barbers and even educators trying their luck at drilling and blasting paydirt. There were some who would panic at the first roar of a drill or run for cover with the first hiss and spurt of a flame from a fuse. We had Howard Midkiff who came from Illinois in the '30's to mine silver ore and drove his partners crazy with his "all the time in the world" igniting of fuse from primed holes. Howard would scratch his privates, wipe his nose, have a sip of coffee as he drifted from fuse to fuse to insure the correct interval between blasts and always reached a safe area before the first concussion.</p>

  <p>Premature explosions were a rarity. We frequently tested sections of fuse and found both white and orange types to be accurately produced. White fuse burned at the rate of 60 seconds to the foot while orange fuse was 15 seconds faster. We used orange fuse in drill holes and white fuse mainly for blasting hangups and pasting large boulders. The only premature explosion during my mining career took the life of Reuben Wesander and severely injured Tommy Thompson in an incline from a vertical raise. Reuben was in the face igniting the notched fuse that made up their round as Tommy waited several feet lower. The blast knocked Tommy from a stull where he fell an additional 80 feet. Subsequent blasts sent rock cascading down on him but he somehow kept his head covered and managed to keep from being buried. There were four unlit fuse on a later inspection which indicated there had been a "runner." So called because the fuse burned faster than normal.</p>

  <p>I have never been afraid of explosives, but I have always handled them carefully and developed a keen respect for the power in a small stick of dynamite. While working from the 850 level of a winze near the Last Chance claim I was to receive several boxes of gelatin dynamite via a can (sump bucket) lowered from an upper level. I was waiting some 10 feet from the bottom when I heard a strange whistling noise then a terrific thud just before being pelted by several 8 inch sticks of dynamite. The fellow loading the boxes had slipped one under the bale of the can and on the first rotation in lowering it fell nearly 300 feet. It was a little unnerving when I thought about it, but there was no time for fear in any event.</p>

  <p>Another time, in the same winze, our hoistman had to lower Hank Slaght and me to the 850 level when he lost pressure to the pneumatic hoist. We had over 40 holes smoking a short distance from the winze and the first blast knocked out the flames of our lamps just as we touched bottom. We rasped them back into life with the flints mounted on reflectors and started for the ladder beside the hoisting compartment. In the brief instant before the next concussion buffeted us I saw that Hank was laughing then his overalls billowed like a balloon and we were again in darkness. He was still laughing when we clambered onto a safe landing in the manway.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-hank-slaght-raymond-ball.jpg" alt="Black-and-white close-up of two men's faces: at left an older man with white hair and a broad smile, and at right a younger man with dark hair leaning in beside him.">
    <figcaption>Henry "Hank" Slaght and Raymond Ball</figcaption>
