  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The title names 66 Raise; the story itself never says where the explosion happened. The fatal blast is the same accident told from other angles elsewhere on this site. In "Echoes from the Thundermug," <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#name-in-society-page-prompts-memory-of-partners">"Name in Society Page Prompts Memory of Partners,"</a> John calls the man who died "Reuben 'Whitey' Wesander" and says Tommy Thompson was later "retrieved from inside a chute near tunnel level where he had been thrown by the force of the blast," while here Tommy is knocked from a stull and falls another 80 feet. The two accounts are kept as printed. The same Echoes page tells a different runaway hoist drop, with Gavin Skinner rather than Hank Slaght, and Echoes' <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#miners-after-world-war-ii-a-unique-breed">"Miners After World War II"</a> puts Wesander and Thompson in 63 raise. This story's opening paragraph on how the Creede veins formed covers the same ground, in different words, as a passage in "Please Go to Hell," <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/the-greenhorn/">"The Greenhorn."</a> "Once Around the Thunder Mug" names "Henry 'Hank' Slaght" as the miner the foreman sent to frame and place square sets in its <a href="/writings/once-around-the-thunder-mug/#origin-of-title">"Origin of Title."</a></em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="mr-lehman-draws-a-joker">Mr. Lehman Draws a Joker</h2>

  <p>During the lean years before economic conditions sparked a rush for more and more tonnage a new hand was placed with a seasoned miner for several weeks to get the feel of working underground. If he proved reliable and could cope with the hazards he would be allowed to handle explosives and start moving up in his apprenticeship.</p>

  <p>My old friend, Dick Lehman, was driving a raise in what was called the West Drift and was asked to take a muscular young fellow as a nipper until he could be put tramming. I.D. Crawford, our foreman, told Dick the boy had worked around mines near Picher, Oklahoma but he was sure he wasn't old enough to have much experience.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image source-doc-image--compact">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-dick-lehman.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a balding man in a short-sleeved collared shirt, seen from the chest up and looking off to one side, with trees behind him.">
    <figcaption>Richard "Dick" Lehman</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>His partner yelled, "Is it caving in?"<br>
    "Only to surface," he replied with a grin.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Dick was pleased with his new partner. He picked up the primers with no show of fear and, when underground, carried a 50# box of DuPont powder from the magazine to a hole near the raise. They had to shovel muck off the bulkhead first then cut hitches in the rock for the heels of timbers that would span the void and provide a drilling platform. Hammer and moils were the tools for chipping the hitches and the lad had a good eye and swing. He appeared eager to join in every phase of the process of blasting rock.</p>

  <p>They were ready to drill. Two planks were nailed to the stulls and a 6" x 6" timber rested against the footwall for added safety. Dick had his steel propped against the end furthest from the drill and a divided box of jack-bits secured on the bulkhead. He advised his partner, "All you need to do now is take the steel when I hand it to you, give me the next length, then change the bit on the starter, just rap it clockwise and it will spin off."</p>

  <p>With the last of the instructions Dick eased the throttle to first notch which fed air to the leg and raised drill, steel and bit to hard rock. We used Ingersol-Rand 58 stopers exclusively. They weighed over a hundred pounds and with three exhaust ports fanning from the off side made one helluva lot of noise. With the bit in tight contact with rock Dick clicked the throttle to full power and cuttings of rhyolite began flying as he opened the water valve to keep dust down and cool the bit. The next thing he was aware of Martin had both arms around his legs, his hat was somewhere in the hole and he was yelling at the top of his lungs. Dick shut the drill off.</p>

  <p>"Oh my God, oh my God," he yelled, "is it caving in Mr. Lehman?"</p>

  <p>"Of course it isn't caving in." Dick responded, "What in hell is wrong with you?"</p>

  <p>"Please, Mr. Lehman, just get me out of here." Martin was still shaking as Dick retrieved his hat and lamp from the muck below the bulkhead then held a hand on his legs as they slowly made their way down the ladder.</p>

  <p>That was Martin's first and last shift in the mines of Creede and I have often wondered what stirring tales he had to tell his children if he became a family man on a farm somewhere in the midwest.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>On the printed page (p. 11) the photograph sits beside the paragraph beginning "Dick was pleased," with the text wrapped around it; it is set here just before that paragraph. Dick Lehman is also named in "Please Go to Hell" as one of the Amethyst Mine's leasers in 1947, in <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/dedication-and-the-amethyst-crew-1947/">"Dedication and The Amethyst Crew '1947,'"</a> which also describes I.D. Crawford, here the foreman. The story does not say whether "Martin" is the young man's given name or his surname.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="forget-about-my-hat-lets-get-outta-here">Forget About My Hat Let's Get Outta Here</h2>

  <p>Two young fellows from Kentucky hired on at the Amethyst Mine. Glen Archer placed them with Freddie Bridsey for their first week on 7 level where Freddie coached them in hooking buckets (switching from empty buckets to loaded buckets for hoisting from pockets) and tramming from stopes where the ore was stored. There are frequent jams in the chutes when large rock become lodged and Freddie had shown one of the boys how to put plasters (mud and dynamite) in the best location to break the rock and loosen the hangups. They were on their own.</p>

  <p>The slender, dark haired, lad chose to work with the buckets and his partner began tramming and dumping ore from a tunnel car into a hole that supplied the ore to be hoisted. A large rock slid from a bulkhead over a stope chute and effectively plugged it. No problem, the Kentucky youth fashioned a plaster with mud, placed a stick of dynamite with primer securely in it, and slapped it on the boulder exactly as Freddie has shown him. He went the other direction and placed a "<em>FIRE IN THE HOLE</em>" sign across the drift then lit the fuse as he retraced his steps. Two minutes is a long wait when you are deep in a mine and nervous. He waited for an interminably long time, or so he thought, decided he had a misfire and walked back to check. He was just at the edge of the chute when a terrific concussion lifted him off his feet and sent his miner's hat spiraling down the drift to the hoisting skids where it clattered downward from side to side and ended up at the feet of the other newcomer. He had heard the explosion and now, with a shattered hardboil blown to him, he was sure his buddy was scattered to hell. He clambered up the manway, skirted the jump hole, and found his friend on hands and knees looking for his carbide lamp. They found the lamp, climbed ladders to 5 level, and ran the remaining mile to the portal. I never saw either again, but Glen told me they collected their pay and left town without changing or paying for board and room</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The last sentence has no closing period in the printed book. "Freddie Bridsey" is printed this way; "Echoes from the Thundermug" and "Please Go to Hell" both spell the surname "Birdsey," and Echoes names a <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#miners-after-world-war-ii-a-unique-breed">Freddie Birdsey</a> tramming and hooking buckets in the Amethyst Mine.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="george-and-the-glory-hole">George and the Glory Hole</h2>

  <p>A fellow from Utah who had worked briefly in a uranium mine was hired as a trammer on 9 level of the vein Les Turnipseed and I were working at a lower level. The shifter liked his attitude and ambition and promoted him to driving 9 level drift. He wasn't a miner by far but worked hard and was tickled pink to learn he had advanced 65 feet his first month. He told Les and me how well he had done and said he was shooting for 100 feet the next tape-up. Les tipped his head back as if figuring and said, "If you made 65 feet you must be getting pretty close to the big cave."</p>

  <p>The kid looked startled and asked,"What's the big cave?"</p>

  <p>Les answered, "It's just a helluva big hole a couple of drifts have run into, guess they've lost several miners in it."</p>

  <p>I picked up the tale then and told George I thought there were just two miners they hadn't found and since the drifts were bulkheaded off no more had gone into it.</p>

  <p>Dick Lehman was our shifter at that time and the next tour of the working faces he found his new miner with a heavy rope tied around his waist anchored to a heavy slusher bucket several feet behind him. He was drilling and when he turned the throttle off Dick asked him why the hell he had himself tied to the bucket?</p>

  <p>George blurted out, "Why didn't you tell me about the big cave, I never would have taken this job if I had known about it?"</p>

  <p>"I don't even know about any big cave, what are you talking about?"</p>

  <p>After the young fellow explained about the conversation with us Dick advised him, "Don't ever believe a word those bastards tell you."</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The book never gives this "George" a surname; he is a newcomer from Utah, not the Kentuckian George Hatfield of "The Big Kentuckian" later in this chapter.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="is-there-a-doctor-in-the-hole">Is There a Doctor in the Hole?</h2>

  <p>Rarely a day passed, in the hole, that someone wasn't nursing a mashed toe or finger or both. A young fellow in the upper Amethyst workings was tramming from a producing stope to a holding raise of the 150 level. His chute had plugged and he was poking at the hang-up with a bar in his right hand as his left hand clutched the side of the tram car. The hang-up broke loose with a gush that sent one sharp slab glancing off the bar to impact with his left hand. He knew it was a bad wound when he took his glove off and he ran to get a miner who had just come out of the stope. Billy could see the hand was a bloody mess and told Benny he would get him outside where he could be driven to town. Benny told him he had to go back and find his glove.</p>

  <p>"Look, Benny, you don't need that damned glove, you're hurt bad and I'm going to get you out."</p>

  <p>"Yes, I do need it,' Benny responded, "my finger's in it."</p>

  <p>And it was. The slab had neatly severed the finger but only partly sliced the pouch of the glove.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-mine-ruins-in-fog.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of collapsed timber mine buildings and a tall, chimney-shaped wooden structure in the foreground, with more weathered buildings fading into fog behind them.">
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>Let the fog roll in, we've served out time<br>
    ghosts of the boom days, coated with rime<br>
    lonely sentinels, still standing guard<br>
    o'er your final words, "Tap 'er light Pard."</p>
  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The photograph and its verse have no title or caption naming the mine. The stray single quotation mark after "need it" in Benny's line is as printed.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="red-the-medicine-man">Red the Medicine Man</h2>

  <p>Melvin "Red" Johnson and Shorty Dunkel were running a short stope near a winze where I worked. As Red was drilling a rock fell and mashed the big toe of his left foot. He took his boot off and found the wound worse than he had first thought. The nail was gone, bone was visible and it was beginning to bleed heavily. Glen archer drove him to town and offered to find someone to take him on to Del Norte and a doctor before he returned to his shifter's duties Red declined, said he would get a neighbor to help get it cleaned up and he would be fine. When I got off shift I met Red near the post office wearing his house slippers with a large hole cut in the top of the left one and a mangled toe protruding. He had a fifth of bourbon and stopped to give me a shot but first poured a healthy drink over the injured toe. He was back in his stope in less than a week which attests to both his durability and the curative powers of good bourbon.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>"Glen archer" is printed with a lowercase "a," and the sentence has no punctuation after "duties"; both are kept as printed. Melvin "Red" Johnson and Shorty Dunkel are paired the same way in "Please Go to Hell," <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/dedication-and-the-amethyst-crew-1947/">"The Amethyst Crew '1947,'"</a> and in Echoes' <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#miners-after-world-war-ii-a-unique-breed">"Miners After World War II."</a></em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="fire-in-the-hole-and-all-over">Fire in the Hole and All Over</h2>

  <p>Walter Waldo had an interesting mishap in the upper Amethyst where Benny lost his finger. Walter was filling shaft buckets to be hoisted to the Sloan level from a lower drift. This required pushing empty buckets on a wooden framed tram car to small chutes to be loaded with ore then returned to the bottom of the winze to be hoisted and emptied at the upper level. Tram buckets are waist high to a normal sized man. When working the chute to load a bucket a man's genitals are often near and at times hanging over the rim as he steps up to slam a chute board down to stop the flow of ore. Walter was in just such a position when a heavy specimen of carbonate lead and silver ore rolled over the bottom board and severely flattened his reproductive organ against cold steel. It didn't only flatten his pecker it mangled it nearly as badly as Red Johnson's left big toe. Walter managed to scale the ladders to the Sloan Tunnel and from there painfully made his way to the portal and the blacksmith shop where Martin Wagner and Art Davis were sharpening steel. When properly apprised of the location and severity of the wound, Art said he would dress it as best he could so the ride to town would be less painful. Martin had the first aid kit ready by the time Walter worked his overalls and long handles down to expose the source of his pain, as Art remarked, "Mi'gawd, that looks like something a dog chewed on for a couple of days and couldn't swallow."</p>

  <p>That wasn't to be the last of Mr. Waldo's humiliation. Art Davis poured a generous dose of iodine on the lacerated appendage and the pair of them were hard pressed to hold Walter for further ministrations. I worked with a miner for several years who suffered the same misfortune as Mr. Waldo; however, the wound to his tallywhacker resulted in an abnormality upon healing that proved beneficial to his social life thereafter. The luck of the Irish.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The book gives "Art Davis" no further identification. "Echoes from the Thundermug" names a mine superintendent, Art Davis, who per family knowledge is no relation to John's uncle <a href="/people/anna-maud-woodruff/#arthur-m-davis">Arthur M. Davis</a>. Whether the Art Davis of this story is that superintendent is not stated.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="the-big-kentuckian">The Big Kentuckian</h2>

  <p>George Hatfield and his growing family traveled west from Kentucky and ended up in Creede. I worked with George, but never learned what prompted him to seek work in the hard rock mines of Colorado. He was a member of the feuding "Hatfield and McCoys" and he never talked about that part of his life either.</p>

  <p>When he was first hired he spent several weeks mucking (shoveling) ore from drifts "tunnels on veins" and tramming the loaded cars to form a train for the mule-skinners or pushing the cars to draw-holes where the ore passed to a lower level. Erick Nelson liked the big Kentuckian and always stopped to visit with him while he was sampling veins in the various workings. After he was adjusted to the altitude and surroundings Erick asked him how he liked his work. "Well, Mr. Nelson," George replied, "In another couple of weeks I reckon I'll be able to eat hay and shit a'walkin'."</p>

  <p>George, James Ott and I were starting a new stope on a high grade silver vein near what was called "The West Drift." I had a terrible hangover one day, had emptied my stomach several times while trying to carry my weight. It was lunch time and I felt improved enough to try a cup of coffee and a few bites of a sandwich. I arrived at our station just as George and Zero opened their pie-cans.</p>

  <p>"Yuh looked purty sick when yuh came in Johnny, feelin' better now?" George asked.</p>

  <p>I told him I thought I might live.</p>

  <p>"I saw yuh puking down the drift, reminded me of a time I was eatin' grits an' molasses, looked at my plate an' saw a half a beetle with one wing still flutterin', knew I'd already swallered the other half, I was near as sick as you sounded."</p>

  <p>I could hear them laughing as I parked my cookies further down a side drift.</p>

  <p>Pete Morowski, a former truck driver, hired on during the time I was stoping with George and he proved to be an amiable fellow. Bill Mathews, a mule-skinner, had quit to return to Texas and Pete took over his job. He told us he was a drinking man, liked good whiskey, and hadn't met a man who could drink him under the table. His stories grew with each telling until George invited him to join a few of the locals for a weekend party. They had just come off shift and Pete said, "I'll get us headed off, by golly." He went into the liquor store and returned with a pint of bourbon, unscrewed the lid and handed it to George. George took a hefty slug, handed the bottle to Uncle Bud who tipped it up for two fast gurgles and he, in turn, passed it to Zero. Zero finished it and gave the empty to Pete.</p>

  <p>There are times when words fail a man. Pete looked at the three of them, who still appeared to be dry, then at his empty bottle. He turned his collar up around his ears and walked out of sight around the corner as George told Uncle Bud and Zero, "Well, if he expected to get a drink, too, he should have bought a quart."</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>George's beetle story is told again, in different words, in "Echoes from the Thundermug," <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-one/#sweet-beetles">"Sweet Beetles,"</a> where the meal is rice and sorghum and the beetle's front half looks up from the bowl. The book's "George Hatfield" is the miner of "Please Go to Hell," in <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/the-hog-killin-and-pass-the-chicken-zero/">"The Hog Killin' and Pass the Chicken, Zero,"</a> where James "Zero" Ott and Zero's Uncle Bud are his companions, too. Bill Mathews is named as a muleskinner in Echoes' <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#miners-after-world-war-ii-a-unique-breed">"Miners After World War II."</a></em></p>
</section>

